      "snippet": {
        "text": "Self-assessment, peer assessments, feedback, portfolios and other tools considerably impact second language learners' performance, motivation and self-regulation abilities.Evidence from the literature indicates that peer assessment and self-assessment can improve students' critical thinking skills, learning performance and motivation while reducing English learning anxiety in different contexts, for example, in classrooms utilising spherical video-based virtual reality [42], in a group-oriented speaking and vocabulary class [43], in negotiation as a social practice in a writing class [44] and the 4/3/2 technique in an English-speaking class [20].These findings suggest that appropriate techniques should be selected based on specific learning contexts.If students are uncomfortable with the techniques, they may feel demotivated to learn.Therefore, it is crucial for learners' and teachers' learning and teaching styles to converge.\n\nTechnology-assisted assessment methods, such as online tests and interactive software, provide a more varied and personalised educational experience for learners.The use of technology can make the assessment process faster, more flexible and interesting, thus stimulating students' learning interest and self-regulation abilities.For instance, Saleh Alharbi et al. [24] observed a substantial level of self-efficacy perceived by learners when utilising Google Forms as an online formative assessment tool.This was evidenced by students' confidence and positive performance, which not only saved class time but also enabled teachers to concentrate on teaching methods and remedial programmes.Similarly, the use of reflective diaries has been found to support self-regulated learning [51].\n\nBoth technology-assisted formative assessment methods and non-technology methods, such as feedback and portfolios, can enhance students' self-regulation abilities.Xiao and Yang [40] discovered that feedback can aid in fostering student self-regulation by involving them in setting goals, generating and responding to feedback, managing resources and undertaking measures to advance their learning.Contreras-Soto et al. [46] confirmed that portfolios help in reducing students' test-related anxiety by providing tailored feedback to each student and allowing them to track their progress.Muho and Leka [23] reported that portfolio assessment altered students' problem-solving approach and promoted autonomy.",
