      "snippet": {
        "text": "Formative assessment, which is the assessment for learning, and summative assessment, which is an assessment of learning, were originally classified by the function they served [40]. Formative assessment is a range of formal and informal assessment procedures conducted by teachers during the learning process to modify teaching and learning activities to improve student attainment. It typically involves qualitative feedback (rather than scores) that focuses on the details of content and performance. The characteristics of formative assessment are [41]: \n\nOngoing: Formative assessments are continuous and are integrated into the learning process, to better support individual student learning. \n\nInteractive: They involve direct interaction between the teacher and the students to provide immediate feedback [42] that can be used to adjust and enhance both teaching and learning strategies. \n\nDevelopmental: The primary purpose is to develop and enhance students' knowledge and skills, rather than to certify mastery or assign grades. \n\nThe use of formative assessment in teaching and learning activities plays a positive role in the development and growth of students in higher education, and practitioners are seeking to explore the underlying logic and strive to further exploit the value of formative assessment. For example, Carter and Prevost [43] have helped students deepen their understanding of many topics in anatomy and physiology based on the core concept of formative assessment -structure-function. As research has progressed, there has also been a realization that formative assessment alone is not enough to have a direct impact on student achievement [44]. \n\nSummative assessment occurs at the end of a program, course, or unit of instruction, and is used to evaluate student learning, skill acquisition, and academic achievement against some standard or benchmark. This type of assessment is typically quantitative, providing a score or grade based on student performance. The characteristics of summative assessment include [41]: \n\nCumulative: Summative assessments provide a way to evaluate learning after an instructional period. \n\nEvaluative: These assessments are used to determine whether students have mastered specific learning goals. \n\nBenchmarked: They often involve comparing educational outcomes against standards or benchmarks. \n\nFormative and summative assessments are complementary [40] (Dixson & Worrell, 2016). While formative assessments help teachers identify areas where students are struggling and adjust instruction accordingly, summative assessments evaluate if the overall goals of the education process have been met.",
