      "snippet": {
        "text": "Providing feedback is an essential part of health professions education (HPE) (Archer, 2010). Although the HPE literature often refers to feedback as if it were generic and welldefined (Ende, 1983;van de Ridder et al., 2008), in practice, what feedback means can vary greatly (Ajjawi & Regehr, 2019;Archer, 2010). For some, feedback is information from haptic laparoscopic trainers (Perrone et al., 2020), for others it is a conversation about defining and improving performance (van de Ridder et al., 2008). Feedback has been defined as a process (Molloy & Boud, 2013), as information (Hattie & Timperley, 2007), as a conversation (Ajjawi & Regehr, 2019), and as a reflective exercise (Moroz et al., 2018(Moroz et al., , 2019)). \n\nThe most obvious challenge stemming from a lack of common understanding of what 'feedback' means is that we cannot be certain that everyone is referring to, or thinking about, the same thing. This is particularly problematic when we aim to synthesize or apply the findings from various studies of feedback. Existing evidence syntheses of feedback in HPE have focused on defining (van de Ridder et al., 2008), re-defining (Ajjawi & Regehr, 2019), examining the outcomes of feedback interventions (Castro et al., 2021;Ivers et al., 2012;Kluger & DeNisi, 1996;Veloski et al., 2006), proposing best practice guidelines (Ende, 1983;Lefroy et al., 2015), theories (Watling, 2014), and frameworks (Brown et al., 2019;Cooke et al., 2018). However, each of these approaches tends to overlook the diverse meanings, interpretations, and complexity of feedback in favour of more straightforward generalizations. Consequently, these studies tend to offer a narrow and limited synthesis of evidence compromising the applicability of their interpretations to the broader landscape of feedback.",
