          "disclaimer": "Notice: This snippet is extracted from the open access paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.1057/s41599-024-03983-6?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.1057/s41599-024-03983-6, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."
        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "This indicates that the timing of feedback can significantly influence the effectiveness of learning and retention strategies. \n\nIn the realm of second/foreign language learning, the majority of research on immediate feedback has been concentrated on vocabulary acquisition (Lu et al., 2021). Nakata (2015), for example, investigated the timing of feedback among Japanese college students learning English vocabulary. By controlling the time interval between feedback and post-tests, Nakata aimed to understand whether immediate or delayed feedback had a more significant impact on vocabulary learning. Interestingly, the study found minimal variation between immediate and delayed feedback in terms of its effectiveness on vocabulary acquisition. Lu et al. (2021) studied the effect of immediate versus delayed feedback on undergraduate students learning different types of knowledge, specifically fun facts about China (situational knowledge) and grammatical patterns (conceptual knowledge). Their findings indicated that immediate feedback led to significantly better performance when learning grammatical patterns (conceptual knowledge). However, there was no significant difference between immediate and delayed feedback when learning situational knowledge (cultural facts). This highlights that the effectiveness of feedback may vary depending on the type of knowledge being learned. \n\nIn many previous studies exploring retrieval practice and feedback, researchers frequently combined the testing effect with feedback, leading to the conclusion that feedback had a beneficial effect. However, it remains unclear whether this observed benefit solely resulted from retrieval practice or if feedback independently contributed to it. More importantly, these studies did not include comparisons between restudying, retesting without feedback, and retesting with feedback groups among EFL students, making it difficult to determine the specific impact of feedback on testing outcomes. To add to the literature on feedback timing and EFL learners, and to add to the generalizability of the effect of feedback with practice tests (and of retrieval practice in general), this study aimed to investigate the effect of both immediate and delayed feedback on long-term retention of prose passage among Saudi EFL students using shortanswer tests administered at three different retention intervals: immediately, one week later, and one month later. Given the strongly supported positive effect of retrieval practice on longterm memory, this study sought to determine whether providing feedback for a brief duration of 10 s could further enhance this advantage. Additionally, I included a restudy control group to facilitate direct comparison between retrieval practice with feedback and restudying conditions.",
