      "snippet": {
        "text": "It is widely understood that particular groups of children and young people will experience differential gains following exposure to the same universal intervention (Greenberg & Abenavoli, 2017). This may be for multiple reasons, including that individuals within a target population will vary in their level of exposure to risk factors that are the foci of the intervention and/or that broader contextual and socialization processes may interact with change mechanisms to favor those with particular characteristics (Farrell et al., 2013;Greenberg & Abenavoli, 2017;Humphrey et al., 2021). \n\nITT analysis cannot account for this inherent heterogeneity in intervention responsiveness, and it is here where subgroup moderator analysis offers considerable utility (Keller, 2019). It is important, however, that subgroups are specified using characteristics measured prior to randomization, such as individual differences at baseline and/or demographic characteristics. In the case of the former, subgroup moderator analysis of our GBG trial data revealed greater reductions in disruptive behavior among participants at higher levels of cumulative risk exposure (Humphrey et al., 2021), in addition to larger long-term gains in pro-social behavior among children with elevated symptoms of conduct problems at baseline (Troncoso & Humphrey, 2021). The first of these findings is particularly noteworthy in the context of the current paper as the subgroup moderator effect was evident specifically in the context of high compliance, underscoring the importance of considering multiple moderators of treatment effects in tandem. With respect to demographic characteristics, a recent trial of the GBG in Canada demonstrated that the intervention reduced conduct problems among boys; improved pro-social behavior and reduced peer problems among children from socio-economically deprived backgrounds; and, improved all outcomes among high-risk children (albeit using point-intime estimation