        "text": "We found 314 records via searches and identified 217 records via reference lists and other sources. We removed 144 duplicates and screened 387 titles and abstracts for eligibility. We read 206 articles and selected 116 papers for narrative synthesis (Figure 2; supplementary material S1). Based on first author affiliation, 57 papers (49%) came from North America, UK and Australasia, 30 papers (26%) from Europe, and 29 papers (25%) from Asia, Africa and Middle East. The breakdown of assessment type was as follows: formative assessment (68 papers), summative assessment ( 16), formative and summative (16), unclear or not stated (16). Overall, 72% of papers examined peer assessment as formative assessment, which is comparable to a recent review where two thirds of studies reviewed focused on formative assessment (Alqassab et al., 2023). \n\nResults are grouped by the categories in Table 1 and evidence strength. Stronger evidence is that drawn from meta-analyses and systematic reviews and/or where evidence for effectiveness exists across multiple assessment and learning contexts. We regard the evidence as mixed where findings are inconsistent across different contexts or where studies report contradictory or inconclusive findings.",
