        "text": "Statistical analyses were performed with SPSS Statistics version 30. \n\nWe described participant characteristics using means and standard deviations for continuous variables and frequency (percentages) for categorical variables. The following four (A to D) models we tested are depicted in Figure 1. We first (A) performed three separate linear regressions in the imaging sample (N = 277) to test the associations between education and episodic memory, education and hippocampal volume, and hippocampal volume and episodic memory (Figure 1A). We then used SPSS PROCESS macro version 3.5 by Andrew F. Hayes 29,30 to perform (B) three separate moderation models, (C) one mediation model, and (D) one moderated mediation model. For analyses involv-ing race/ethnicity as a moderator, we excluded those who self-reported their race/ethnicity as \"Multiple\" (n = 16), \"Other\" (n = 1), or \"Refused\" to respond (n = 1), resulting in 259 participants. In testing moderations (Figure 1B), we examined whether race/ethnicity moderated the associations among education, hippocampal volume, and episodic memory. Since evidence of an association is not required to test the moderation of an association, 31 we tested for moderations regardless of whether a significant main association was found. In a simple mediation (Figure 1C), we first tested if hippocampal volume mediated the association of educational attainment (independent variable) with verbal episodic memory (outcome variable). If race/ethnicity moderated any of the aforementioned three main associations among education, hippocampal volume, and memory, it was included as a moderator for the specific path(s) in the moderated mediation model (post hoc model: \n\nFigure 1D). The moderated mediation model was run in SPSS PRO-CESS, with the model number determined post hoc depending on the path that was significantly moderated by race/ethnicity. Race/ethnicity was coded as a multicategory variable (indicator) as the modera