      "snippet": {
        "text": "The dataset can be accessed from https://figshare.com /s/3ae5b09234338c01263a. The features used in the dataset was extracted from publicly available datasets provided by the National Center for Education Statistics (NCES) through their website https://nces.ed.gov/programs/coe/indicator/cgg /students-with-disabilities. This study considered a dataset designed to model heterogeneous learning profiles of students with different disabilities. The dataset includes attributes that enable one to create a comprehensive picture of a student's particulars including age, intellect, and overall performance. A student ID was assigned to each student, which contained all the children's data separately, thus enabling the study to be clear and effective. The dataset encompasses school-aged children and adolescents, particularly those aged 5-18 years, and depicts various advances in and levels of educational achievement of learners. \n\nRegarding the male-female ratio, the dataset comprises 'Male,' 'Female' and 'Non-Binary' which are broad categories providing a holistic attempt to establish adaptive learning requirements of all genders. It is important to note this demographic diversity when assessing the impact of adaptive learning on various groups. The participants in the dataset were classified according to the nature of their learning disabilities. These include dyslexia; ADHD; autistic spectrum disorder; and visual, hearing, and intellectual disabilities. These types were selected because of their significance in educational performance and their effect on students' learning activities. \n\nThe dataset consists of a variety of features that are believed to capture the essential characteristics of students' cognitive and academic performance. An essential feature is reading comprehension score, which defines how well students interpret and understand written words. This score is particularly relevant in examining the learning patterns of students with learnin