      "snippet": {
        "text": "Formative assessment requires the collection of information about student progress, difficulties, and successes to improve teaching or learning (Black & Wiliam, 2009;Bloom et al., 1981). Performance data can be interactions in a classroom (e.g., question and answer), but also includes more formal diagnostic testing (Brown et al., 2012) or analysis of errors made in classroom or home practice (Bejar, 1984). From these kinds of performance data, teachers can formatively adjust their classroom instruction, feedback processes, and pedagogical activities used with students (Lai & Schildkamp, 2016;Palm et al., 2023) to meet the students' learning needs. Naturally, a formative approach to assessment requires teachers having diagnostic assessment tools (Säfström et al., 2024), as well as time to devise, administer, and respond to assessed results (Brown & Hattie, 2012), policies that prioritise using assessment formatively rather than solely for administrative or summative purposes (Harris & Brown, 2009), and teacher commitment to using assessment formatively for improvement (Brown, 2004). \n\nFurther, considering the importance of the teacher's active role in using assessment data formatively, teachers' AL and FL matter to the efficacy of these processes. Multiple frameworks highlight the importance of teachers' beliefs and attitudes toward any phenomenon (e.g., Ajzen's (1991) Theory of Planned Behaviour (TPB); Fives and Buehl's (2012) model of teacher beliefs). Teacher beliefs about the proper role of assessment and feedback are not universal; they are context bound by the policy and practice framework in which they are employed (Bonner, 2016;Brown & Harris, 2009;Fulmer et al., 2015).",
