      "snippet": {
        "text": "This study explored tertiary EFL teachers' CBA practices in terms of their alignment with LOA culture. The investigation also explored the nature of the relationship between assessment practices as articulated and practiced in the real-time classroom situation. The interpretation of findings revealed three major themes: diagnostic and instructional purpose of assessment; misalignment of assessment practices with learning-oriented assessment culture; and complexity, contradiction, and incongruity between reported and practiced classroom assessments. The participants' reported use of classroom assessments for knowing their learners' learning progress and identifying the strengths and weaknesses of their instruction highlight the diagnostic purpose of assessment, an important aspect of the formative assessment framework. Utilizing diagnostic assessment evidence for learning through continuing formative feedback, which is focused, instructive, and timely, is considered an essential learning support mechanism in the formative assessment process (Torrance, 2017; Tukan & Mukarto, 2024;Yang & Carless, 2013). Deeper learning, that is, acquiring higher-order thinking skills, takes place if classroom assessment is used as a dynamic process of using assessment data to continuously improve students' learning based on four elements: learning goals are made clear to students, in-class assessment activities seamlessly integrated with instruction are conducted, students' learning progress is constantly monitored followed by focused and timely feedback, and students are involved in self and peer-assessment tasks (Black & William, 2018;Heritage, 2010;Wu et al., 2021). Here, the quality of interactive formative feedback has an important place in the learning process. However, the findings of this study reveal issues with during and post-assessment feedback, which is contrary to the essence of formative assessment framework. This could be attributed to institutional policy regarding feedback and the practical aspect of conducting assessments in large classes and contexts with a heavy teacher workload. These feedback-related challenges and practicalities due to contextual educational policy dynamics are highlighted by Boud and Molloy (2013), who contend that feedback processes in higher education are mostly challenging to manage and carry out effectively, falling short of their goal to significantly influence learner progress in learning.",
